-- title:
--   Characters of the operator algebra give eigenvectors after base change
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $M$ an $R$-module (an additive commutative group with an $R$-action) which is finitely generated over $R$, let $F$ be a field equipped with an $R$-algebra structure, and let $t \colon \iota \to \operatorname{End}_R(M)$ be a family of $R$-linear endomorphisms of $M$, indexed by an arbitrary type $\iota$, such that $t_i$ and $t_j$ commute for all $i, j$. Write $A = \operatorname{adjoin}_R(\{t_i : i \in \iota\}) \subseteq \operatorname{End}_R(M)$ for the $R$-subalgebra generated by the range of $t$, and let $\chi \colon A \to F$ be a homomorphism of $R$-algebras. The assertion is that there exists an element $y$ of the base change $F \otimes_R M$ with $y \neq 0$ such that for every index $i$ the base-changed endomorphism $\operatorname{id}_F \otimes t_i$ of $F \otimes_R M$ satisfies $(\operatorname{id}_F \otimes t_i)(y) = \chi(t_i) \cdot y$, where $t_i$ is regarded as an element of $A$ via the inclusion of the generators in the adjoined subalgebra, and $\chi(t_i) \in F$ acts on $F \otimes_R M$ by scalar multiplication. Thus a single nonzero vector realises the whole system of eigenvalues prescribed by $\chi$.
--
--   This is the standard comparison between $F$-valued characters of a commutative Hecke-type operator algebra acting on a finitely generated module and systems of simultaneous eigenvalues in the base-changed module, in the form used by Deligne–Serre and by Ash–Stevens (the converse implication being immediate). It is used in the construction of mod $p$ eigensystems on the relevant cohomology: the statement is invoked when passing from a character of the algebra generated by commuting Hecke operators to an actual eigenvector for all of them over a field of characteristic $p$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Module_exists_ne_zero_forall_baseChange_eq_smul_of_algHom.lean

